[Unit]
Description=ZeroGEX gamma-flip resolution check (alerts when the flip is left unpublished)
Documentation=https://github.com/zerogex-options/zerogex-oa
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

# WHY THIS EXISTS. The sibling carry check asks whether a five-minute bar had
# a gex_summary row at all. This asks the opposite question about the rows
# that ARE there: the row exists, the cycle ran, and the resolver declined to
# publish a flip. Persisting NULL is the correct behaviour, and it is also
# completely silent -- every consumer renders nothing, and on the NinjaTrader
# panel the Flip line is simply an em dash.
#
# That silence cost us five weeks. SPX ran blank for the whole session from
# 2026-08-07 to 2026-08-17 and NDX from 2026-08-07 to 2026-09-09, and the
# first anybody heard of it was a beta tester's screenshot. Nothing was
# watching, so nothing was reported. This unit is the thing that was missing.
#
# Requires `make alert-template-install` and a backend in /etc/zerogex/alert.env
# (ALERT_BACKEND=resend + ALERT_EMAIL_TO for email). Without them the
# dispatcher falls back to ALERT_BACKEND=stderr and the alert lands in
# `journalctl -u zerogex-alert@*` -- degraded, but never a failed install.
OnFailure=zerogex-alert@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=ubuntu
Group=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/zerogex-oa

# ANALYTICS_UNDERLYINGS comes from here, so the check covers exactly the
# symbols the analytics engine writes -- add an underlying to the engine and
# it is covered on the next fire, with no unit to edit. Which matters here:
# the outage above was cash-index-only, and a unit with a hardcoded symbol
# list is exactly how the next one hides.
Environment="PATH=/home/ubuntu/zerogex-oa/venv/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"
EnvironmentFile=/home/ubuntu/zerogex-oa/.env

# SESSIONS=2 and EDGES=1: alert on the CHANGE, not on the state.
#
# An edge needs a predecessor, so a one-session window reports nothing at all.
# The first session in any window has a known STATE and an unknown CHANGE, and
# that distinction is the whole mechanism.
#
# WHY NOT LEVEL-BASED. On 2026-09-21..23 NDX was blank for three straight
# sessions and the resolver was right every time: the monthly opex on 09-18
# left a book at 3.16 calls per put, long gamma across the whole band, crossing
# 27% to 37% below spot. Level-based, this unit emailed about it roughly
# twenty-one times, and a monitor people learn to ignore is the state the
# 2026-07..09 blackout hid in for five weeks.
#
# Two attempts to excuse the harmless case by deciding WHY the flip was dark
# both failed on real data, and both stay out of here deliberately:
#
#   IGNORE_REASONS=BEYOND_MAX_DISTANCE -- that code is reported both for a bug
#     in our own DTE weighting and for a genuinely long-gamma book.
#   IGNORE_RAW_BEYOND=8 -- the ungated crossing sat 0% to 21% from spot when
#     the fault was ours and 27% to 35% when it was the market. At 8 it excused
#     26 of the 31 breaching blackout sessions.
#
# Nothing in the stored row separates them, so this stops trying. A bug
# starting and a regime starting are both worth exactly one look.
#
# REPLAYED BEFORE INSTALL, over 2026-07-01..09-23. Four alerts across 43
# sessions against roughly two hundred level-based, and it catches every onset
# including the one that took five weeks and a customer to find:
#
#     2026-07-29   WENT DARK     0m -> 332m
#     2026-08-03   WENT DARK     0m -> 221m     the five-week blackout begins
#     2026-09-11   WENT DARK     6m -> 263m
#     2026-09-21   WENT DARK     0m -> 389m     the post-opex regime begins
#
# NOTE ON RE-ALERTING. Within the onset day the comparison stays "yesterday was
# publishing, today is dark", so fires after the crossing repeat it: at most
# six emails on the day something breaks, then silence while it stays broken.
# Proportionate on the day it matters, quiet on the days it does not.
#
# KNOWN TRADE, stated rather than hidden. An edge speaks once. Miss that alert
# and a long outage stays silent afterwards, because there is no second change
# to report until it recovers. The backstop is a low-frequency digest of what
# is currently dark, which does not exist yet. Until it does, the level view is
# one command away:
#     make gamma-flip-resolution-healthcheck SESSIONS=1
#
# Exits 0 when nothing newly went dark, 1 when something did, 2 on DB error. A
# nonzero exit marks the unit failed, surfaces in `systemctl --failed`, logs
# the offending symbol and session, and fires the OnFailure hook above.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/make -C /home/ubuntu/zerogex-oa gamma-flip-resolution-healthcheck SESSIONS=2 EDGES=1

# One indexed range scan and a rollback per symbol-session.
TimeoutStartSec=120

# Security
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=read-only
ReadWritePaths=/home/ubuntu/zerogex-oa

# Logging
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=zerogex-oa-gamma-flip-resolution
